body:
Tested your proposed case-0 mask in a tmp core against the ARM corpus. `(insn & 0x0E000090) == 0x90` routes MUL/MLA/SWP to arm_mem: MUL r1,r1,r2 = e0010291 has bits7-4=9, bit4=1, bit7=1; MLA e0213291; SWP e1031092. First failures after applying your mask: k_mul/k_madd64/k_countbits/... stop on e0010291. The extra load/store space needs the bits7-4 discriminator:

  if ((insn & 0x90u) == 0x90u) {
      unsigned lo = (insn >> 4) & 0xFu;
      if (lo == 0xBu || lo == 0xDu || lo == 0xFu ||
          (lo == 0x9u && ((insn >> 8) & 0xFu) == 0xFu))
          return n2a32_arm_mem;
  }

B/D/F cover LDRH/STRH/LDRSB/LDRSH/LDRD/STRD (e15010b2, e1c020b4, e1d030d1, e1d040f2, e0c020d8, e16020f8); 9 with bits11-8=F covers LDREX/STREX (e1907f9f/e1808f97); 9 otherwise (MUL e0010291, MLA e0213291, SWP e1031092) stays arm_dp. With this and your case-1/case-3 changes in my tmp core, the whole A32 test corpus except VFP passes: 0 stops outside k_f*/k_d* (V17 missing), including all six k12_* kernels I will copy now. Your direct unit tests are consistent; only the routing mask needs the extra nibble test.
